ID: Ch5-Intro
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch5_wielder,1) if (v_ch4_dave_defeat == 1 && v_ch4_ending == 5)
set(v_is_dead,0)
achievement("Stat device wielder",v_ac_ch5_wielder)
#if(v_ch4_ending == 1) {

"Daren!" I shout. "Daren, can you hear me?"

"Barry? Stay put! I'm on my way!"

I have no intention of moving. My kneecaps throb with every pulse of blood, sharp and hot. I stay flat on the dirt so I don't give the joints an excuse to buckle again. Eiden certainly left his mark.

"Barry! What happened?" Daren crashes through the brush, shield up, scanning the clearing.

"Nothing major. Just a friendly send-off from our host."

"Eiden," Daren growls, knuckles whitening on the hilt of his sword.

He drops to one knee and presses his palm near my leg. A brilliant white glow spills between his fingers, washing through the joint with a steady, soothing heat. The throbbing drains away until the cartilage knits tight and whole. I push up from the forest floor without so much as a twinge. Spellcraft like this is something else entirely—most healers back home could barely patch a sprained ankle without sweating through their robes.

"Where is the stillwater?" he asks.

"Gone. But before he left, he turned Cutthroat Dave and his entire crew into ash. Fire magic, combustion, instantaneous teleportation—all of it without drawing a hard breath. Dave made the mistake of confusing him for me, and I gave him a little nudge."

"Good riddance. But how did I end up flat on my back?"

"You don't remember? He flicked two fingers and dropped you where you stood."

"What? No—that's impossible. I have layered sleep wards woven into this armor. I should be nearly immune, or at the very least conscious enough to resist! And fire magic? He held me down with pure compressed air when we first crossed paths. Mastering even a single element takes half a lifetime!"

He falls silent, staring at the spot where Eiden vanished. Then he turns back to me, something shifting behind his eyes.

"Barry. When we met yesterday, I told you I would walk with you until we reached safer ground. That was the arrangement." He pauses, pressing one fist against the pommel of his sword. "But you just traded your own body to a being who could unmake the both of us—for my sake. That is not a stranger's gamble. That is..." He shakes his head, a rough exhale escaping through his teeth. "I am not going to pretend this is still a temporary escort. If you will have me, I am with you until this tournament ends. Not as repayment. As a choice."

}
#if(v_ch4_ending == 2) {

"Daren!"

Silence.

"Hey, Daren!"

Daren jerks upright, blinking hard as he scrambles for his sword.

"What happened? Where's the stillwater?"

"That's the second time you've asked me those exact words. He's gone. Vanished the second he put you under."

"Put me under? No—that's impossible. I have layered sleep wards woven into this armor. I should be nearly immune, or at the very least conscious enough to resist!"

"I'm telling you what I saw. I don't have an explanation either."

"Would you two mind telling me who that stillwater was?" Kate asks, stepping up beside us with her arms crossed. "And what trade he was talking about?"

Daren and I lay out the details of our run-in with Eiden from the previous day.

When we finish, Kate stares into the trees, turning the pieces over in her head.

"Let me get this straight," she says. "Barry, you traded our lives for a single message."

"That's the deal."

"And we're supposed to deliver it to anyone in robes wearing a brooch shaped like a hawk clutching the sun."

"Right. Does the description ring a bell?"

"Yes," she says, her gaze immediately dropping to the dirt.

Another secret. It's written in every line of her posture.

"They're an underground faction," she continues, voice dropping. "They rarely show their faces in daylight. Nobody knows who runs them or what they actually do—most people on the continent don't even know the name. But the rumor is they answer directly to the kings of Varathia."

"Backed by the kings?" Daren's brow furrows. "What does Eiden want with royal lapdogs? You just tell them to remember the still winter, right? What is that supposed to mean?"

He pauses, then glances at me—something settling behind his expression.

"Barry. You talked a stillwater into releasing all three of us for the price of a sentence. I said I would walk with you until safer ground—I think we are well past that now. Whatever comes next, I am in."

}
#if(v_ch4_ending == 4) {

"Barry!" Daren calls from the tree line, heavy boots crunching over pine needles.

"Over here," I answer.

He pushes through the branches, head turning as he searches the clearing. "Where's the cutthroat?"

"If you look near that boulder, you can still see the scorched patch where he used to be."

"Ashes? Don't tell me that device of yours grants fire magic now."

"Not me. Eiden came back and incinerated Dave the second he drew a blade on me. Said it wouldn't do for his messenger to die inside the post office."

"Wait—fire magic? He pinned me to the stone with air when we first met. Mastering even a single element takes half a lifetime!"

He scrubs a hand over his bald scalp, jaw tight. "Barry—you walked up to a being who dropped me with two fingers, and negotiated. Alone. Unarmed." He meets my eyes. "I said I would stay with you until safer ground. I think we are well past that arrangement. Whatever comes next, I am in."

}
#if(v_ch4_ending == 5 && v_ch4_dave_defeat != 1 || v_ch4_ending == 3) {

"Daren!"

Silence.

"Hey, Daren!"

Daren jerks upright, blinking hard as he scrambles for his sword.

"What happened? Where's the stillwater?"

"That's the second time you've asked me those exact words. He's gone. Vanished the second he put you under."

"Put me under? No—that's impossible. I have layered sleep wards woven into this armor. I should be nearly immune, or at the very least conscious enough to resist!"

"I'm telling you what I saw. I don't have an explanation either. Regardless, we need to wake Kate. She needs to hear this, and we're long overdue for a strategy meeting."

The two of us head for the cave to wake Kate from her slumber.

}
#if(v_ch4_dave_defeat == 1 && v_ch4_ending == 5) {

"Daren!"

Silence.

"Hey, Daren!"

Daren jerks upright, blinking hard as he scrambles for his sword.

"What happened? Where's the stillwater?"

"That's the second time you've asked me those exact words. He's gone. Vanished the second he put you under."

"Put me under? No—that's impossible. I have layered sleep wards woven into this armor. I should be nearly immune, or at the very least conscious enough to resist!"

"I'm telling you what I saw. I don't have an explanation either. Regardless, we need to wake Kate. She needs to hear this, and we're long overdue for a strategy meeting."

"Hold on, Barry. Let me see that stat device of yours first."

"What for? What's on your mind?"

"Just hand it over for a second. I want to test something."

I pass him the device. He turns it over in his rough hands, squinting at the casing.

"Are you going to do someth—"

"Quiet."

He scoops a jagged stone from the ground and hurls it at a nearby pine. The stone strikes with a dull clatter and drops into the needles, leaving nothing more than a faint graze on the bark.

"Just as I suspected," Daren says. "Look at that mark. You'd never know I was holding an enchanted booster. But that throw of yours earlier slammed a grown man clean off his feet and cracked the trunk behind him. How are you pulling that kind of force out of this thing?"

"I don't know. I assumed it drew on the ambient magical currents in the air. Maybe something about my own constitution interacts with it. Without knowing the internal enchantment, it's impossible to say."

"Hmm." He hands the device back. "No use standing around guessing. We'll sort it out later. Let's get Kate."

The two of us head for the cave to wake Kate from her slumber.

}
