"Backed by the kings?" Daren's brow furrows. "What does Eiden want with royal lapdogs? You just tell them to remember the still winter, right? What is that supposed to mean?"

}
choice(""It sounds like a threat."", Ch5-Threat, v_current_scene = Ch5-Threat)
choice(""It sounds like a coded message."", Ch5-Coded, v_current_scene = Ch5-Coded)


ID: Ch5-Threat
TEXT:

#if(v_ch2_human_reason == 1) {
"It sounds like a threat," I say. "It matches everything we've seen of Eiden so far—hurl us into his enemies' teeth and enjoy the slaughter from the sidelines."

Kate nods.

"I agree," she says. "He wouldn't leave us breathing out of sentiment for old companions. A veiled warning to rivals makes far more sense. I don't know what the still winter refers to, but I'd wager steel clears leather the instant those words leave our mouths."

"So our choices are an entire royal cabal, or an invincible stillwater who can turn us into mist with a flick of his wrist. On the bright side, there's no deadline. If we steer clear of anyone in hawk robes until the tournament ends, we might actually survive this."

"Steady on," Daren cuts in. "We can't assume hostility from phrasing alone. And our immediate priority should be figuring out how to neutralize Eiden, not obsessing over his errand!"

"And what's the plan for that?" Kate's voice turns sharp. "He bypassed your sleep wards in half a second, and your defenses are sturdier than mine. That single spell would incapacitate the three of us, to say nothing of the rest of his arsenal."

"So we just roll over and run his errands like obedient dogs?"

"Nobody said that. I'm saying we lack the intelligence to construct a viable strategy. Until we uncover a vulnerability in his technique, we should make the most of the reprieve Barry negotiated, however long it lasts."

I catch the phrasing and file it away. The reprieve Barry negotiated. Not "the deal you stumbled into." Not "the mess you made." She framed it as a deliberate tactical move—as if my gambit with Eiden had been a calculated play rather than blind panic dressed up in bravado. Coming from Kate, that is as close to a compliment as I have ever heard.

"Perhaps you're right. Let's hope that window gives us time to find an opening."

"Kate," I say, nodding toward the mouth of the cave. "Do you have a minute?"

She blinks, caught off guard.

"Of course," she says, and follows me outside.

As we pass, Daren catches my eye, winks, and gives me a broad thumbs-up. Leave it to Daren to read romance into a tactical inquiry. His grasp of social cues is truly breathtaking.

"I'll be direct," I tell her once we clear the rock overhang. "What is the Beacon of Hope?"

}
#if(v_ch2_human_reason == 2) {
"It sounds like a threat," I say. "It matches everything we've seen of Eiden so far—hurl us into his enemies' teeth and enjoy the slaughter from the sidelines."

Kate nods.

"I agree," she says. "He wouldn't leave us breathing out of sentiment for old companions. A veiled warning to rivals makes far more sense. I don't know what the still winter refers to, but I'd wager steel clears leather the instant those words leave our mouths."

