I exhale slowly.

"I lied to you yesterday about why I came to Varathia. I wasn't searching for medicinal herbs. I came of my own free will because I want to win this tournament. Ever since I was a child, I've wanted to become a mage, and the Magium can grant that. I'm sorry for misleading you."

"And the story about your sister? A fabrication as well?"

"Yes."

"Oh," Daren says.

His eyes go flat and cold.

"Well. Now I know."

"Now you know."

Kate offers a faint, approving nod from the shadows, but Daren's expression remains stone-still. I leave him to process the revelation and step back toward the open air.

}
choice("Continue", Ch5-Scream, v_current_scene = Ch5-Scream)


ID: Ch5-No-admit
TEXT:

Whatever Kate thinks, there's no telling how Daren would react to learning I'm aiming for the same prize as everyone else. Admitting I've been lying since we met would only shatter what trust we have left. At this stage, staying the course is the only viable play.

I lean back against the outer rock of the cave, turning the pieces over. Contestants slaughter each other in the brush, the organizers withhold the basic objectives, and an ancient stillwater wanders the woods looking for amusement. What is he actually after?

choice("Continue", Ch5-Scream, v_current_scene = Ch5-Scream)


ID: Ch5-Scream
TEXT:

#if(v_ch5_admit_lie == 0) {
A woman's scream cuts through the distant trees, shrill and abrupt. In the same breath, a luminous glyph ignites in the air before my eyes—the tournament's automated warning rune, reminding contestants of the strict non-aggression decree protecting Varathian locals.

The sound carries from a fair distance, but faint, jagged pulses of magic ripple from that direction. Monster signatures—small, fragmented, and weak. Most likely a small pack of goblins.

The tournament charter remains vague regarding rescues. Non-interference in local affairs is the stated policy, yet unlike direct assault against citizens, it carries no automated binding enchantment.

Pragmatically, staying put carries zero risk. Any intervention gambles with tournament standing. But then—

The woman screams again, higher this time, terror fraying the edge of her voice.

}
#if(v_ch5_admit_lie == 1) {
As I step into the morning light, a woman's scream cuts through the distant trees, shrill and abrupt. In the same breath, a luminous glyph ignites in the air before my eyes—the tournament's automated warning rune, reminding contestants of the strict non-aggression decree protecting Varathian locals.

The sound carries from a fair distance, but faint, jagged pulses of magic ripple from that direction. Monster signatures—small, fragmented, and weak. Most likely a small pack of goblins.

The tournament charter remains vague regarding rescues. Non-interference in local affairs is the stated policy, yet unlike direct assault against citizens, it carries no automated binding enchantment.

Pragmatically, staying put carries zero risk. Any intervention gambles with tournament standing. But then—

The woman screams again, higher this time, terror fraying the edge of her voice.

}
choice("Ignore her", Ch5-Ignore, v_current_scene = Ch5-Ignore, v_ch5_ignore_scream = 1)
choice("Call Daren and head for the rescue", Ch5-Rescue, v_current_scene = Ch5-Rescue, v_ch5_ignore_scream = 0)


ID: Ch5-Ignore
TEXT:

#if(v_ch5_admit_lie == 0) {
I didn't come to Varathia to play the hero. Winning the tournament comes first. If intervening carries even the slightest risk of disqualification, staying out of it is the only logical choice.

"What was that sound?" Daren demands, striding toward the cave entrance with his hand already resting on his hilt.

"One of the locals," I say. "Surrounded by monsters. Probably goblins."

"By the gods! Why didn't you shout the instant you heard her?"

"The tournament charter explicitly forbids interference in local affairs."

"Do you honestly think anyone will penalize us for saving a woman's life? What's come over you, Barry? This isn't like you."

He barrels past me into the trees, heavy boots tearing through the undergrowth.

Kate, stepping up from the shadows of the cave, clears her throat.

"If it matters, you made the rational call. Flouting the non-interference decree is a needless gamble."

"Thanks. But it's too late for caution now—we can't leave Daren to charge in alone. Move."

We sprint after him, crashing through the pines, but by the time we break into the clearing, the woods are quiet.

"Look who decided to join the fray," Daren says, leaning casually against a boulder.

"Where are the goblins?" I ask, scanning the surrounding brush.

"Scattered the moment they saw my plate. Not the stoutest fighters. I didn't even have to clear leather."

When I turn toward the woman, the sight of her stops me cold. Long auburn hair cascades over the shoulders of an auburn dress, practical and dust-streaked from the trail. She has a slender waist, soft, delicate features that contrast sharply with Kate's guarded severity, and warm light-brown eyes that carry an immediate, unvarnished kindness. Mid-twenties, holding an herbalist's woven satchel smelling of damp earth and crushed mint. She brushes dirt from her fingers with quiet composure, straightening her posture, and dips into an elegant, gracious bow.

"Thank you all so much for your help," she says, her voice smooth and remarkably steady. "I don't know what I would have done without you!"

}
