choice(""Daren is the one you should thank, not us."", Ch5-Humble, v_current_scene = Ch5-Humble)
choice(""We only did what anyone else would have done in our stead."", Ch5-Stead, v_current_scene = Ch5-Stead)


ID: Ch5-Humble
TEXT:

"Daren is the one you should thank, not us," I say, nodding toward the armored healer. "He's the one who charged ahead despite the tournament's non-interference rule."

"I am well aware," she says, a polite, knowing smile touching her lips. "Yet that doesn't change the fact that both of you followed your companion into the woods regardless, and for that, you have my gratitude. My name is Rose. It's a pleasure to meet you all."

"If I may ask," Kate cuts in, stepping forward with her arms crossed, "what were you doing out here in the wilderness alone?"

"Well, miss, err..."

"Kate," Kate says with a short gesture toward her own chest, then indicates me and Daren in turn, supplying our names without lingering on formalities.

Rose offers a warm nod.

"Miss Kate, I was searching for rare medicinal flora native to this ridge. An outbreak of oildrip has struck my hometown, and as an herbalist, I know which roots and leaves produce the remedy."

"What is oildrip?" Kate asks.

"Ah, I forgot the ailment doesn't exist across the ocean. The physicians remain uncertain of the root cause, but patients develop a burning fever, violent coughing fits, and sweat as thick and dark as lamp oil. Some possess natural resistance, but those without it are quarantined in isolated wards and left to waste away. The only known cure requires extracts from plants that grow exclusively along these hills."

"Why come yourself?" Kate presses, eyes narrowing slightly. "Are your own kin infected? Why not hire guards to escort you?"

choice("Continue", Ch5-Creator, v_current_scene = Ch5-Creator)


ID: Ch5-Stead
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch5_shameless,1)
achievement("Shameless",v_ac_ch5_shameless)
"We only did what anyone else would have done in our stead."

Rose tilts her head, a knowing amusement dancing in her eyes.

"Indeed. Most contestants would have done exactly that—left me to the goblins rather than risk a reprimand from the tournament judges."

The heat rises straight to my ears.

"Yet you chose to follow your friend into danger anyway," she adds, her tone softening, "and for that, you have my gratitude. My name is Rose. It's a pleasure to meet you all."

"If I may ask," Kate cuts in, stepping forward with her arms crossed, "what were you doing out here in the wilderness alone?"

"Well, miss, err..."

"Kate," Kate says with a short gesture toward her own chest, then indicates me and Daren in turn, supplying our names without lingering on formalities.

Rose offers a warm nod.

"Miss Kate, I was searching for rare medicinal flora native to this ridge. An outbreak of oildrip has struck my hometown, and as an herbalist, I know which roots and leaves produce the remedy."

"What is oildrip?" Kate asks.

"Ah, I forgot the ailment doesn't exist across the ocean. The physicians remain uncertain of the root cause, but patients develop a burning fever, violent coughing fits, and sweat as thick and dark as lamp oil. Some possess natural resistance, but those without it are quarantined in isolated wards and left to waste away. The only known cure requires extracts from plants that grow exclusively along these hills."

"Why come yourself?" Kate presses, eyes narrowing slightly. "Are your own kin infected? Why not hire guards to escort you?"

choice("Continue", Ch5-Creator, v_current_scene = Ch5-Creator)


ID: Ch5-Rescue
TEXT:

There's no time to debate tournament regulations. A civilian is in immediate danger.

"Daren! On your feet!"

"What is it?" He looks up, alert.

"One of the locals is trapped by monsters just past the ridge. If we move now, we can reach her before they close in!"

Daren's face hardens. He draws his broadsword with a clean scrape of steel.

"Lead the way."

We sprint through the trees, the underbrush whipping against our boots. As we close the distance, the faint magical signatures sharpen into four distinct pulses. They have her surrounded in a tight circle, though no blood has been drawn yet.

Breaking through the thicket, the scene snaps into focus. Goblins. Four feet tall, wiry, with mottled green skin, jutting lower fangs, crooked noses, and pointed ears. Despite their classification as beasts, goblins maintain structured clans, craft crude iron tools, and coordinate with tactical intelligence.

choice("Continue", Ch5-Goblins, v_current_scene = Ch5-Goblins)


ID: Ch5-Goblins
TEXT:

set(v_ch5_goblins,0) if (v_ancient_languages == 3 || v_ancient_languages < 3)
#if(v_ancient_languages < 3) {

The moment they spot us, the lead goblin barks a guttural order in a harsh, rolling tongue, and all four scramble into the thick undergrowth. They clearly have no stomach for a fair fight against armed humans.

With the monsters gone, I turn toward the woman to ensure she took no harm. The sight of her stops me cold. Long auburn hair cascades over the shoulders of an auburn dress, practical and dust-streaked from the trail. She has a slender waist, soft, delicate features that contrast sharply with Kate's guarded severity, and warm light-brown eyes that carry an immediate, unvarnished kindness. Mid-twenties, holding an herbalist's woven satchel smelling of damp earth and crushed mint. She brushes dirt from her fingers with quiet composure, straightening her posture, and dips into an elegant, gracious bow.

"Thank you all so much for your help," she says, her voice smooth and remarkably steady. "I don't know what I would have done without you! My name is Rose. It's a pleasure to meet you."

}
#if(v_ancient_languages == 3) {

The moment they spot us, the lead goblin barks a harsh command. The words click instantly into place in my head—an ancient dialect deciphered by the stat device's translation matrix.

"Human warriors!" he shouts. "Leave the female! Fall back!"

"Hold," I call out in their own tongue.

The leader freezes, ears twitching in disbelief. "The human speaks our speech?"

"I do. Why are you attacking an unarmed traveler?"

"We are starving, human! We did not mean to slit her throat. We only wanted the food in her satchel."

"Why are you robbing people in the wild? Shouldn't your clan provide for you?"

"The clan cast us out two suns ago," the goblin rasps, shoulders sagging. "We had no coin to pay the yearly tribute. We are exiles now, scavenging scraps. We cannot live off the forest—we do not know which berries bring life and which bring death."

"How much was the tribute for the four of you?"

"Two copper pieces."

Two coppers. Barely the price of a cheap loaf of bread. Goblins are classed as monsters, cutting them off from lawful trade or human employment, and black-market smugglers charge exorbitant rates. It's no surprise a couple of common coins mean survival to them.

I have coin to spare in my pouch. The question is whether I hand it over to thieves who had a woman cornered moments ago.

}
choice(""All in the day's work!"", Ch5-Work, v_current_scene = Ch5-Work) if (v_ancient_languages < 3)
choice(""You were lucky that we managed to make it in time."", Ch5-Lucky, v_current_scene = Ch5-Lucky) if (v_ancient_languages < 3)
choice(""No problem. We love helping people. But we also accept donations!"", Ch5-Donations, v_current_scene = Ch5-Donations) if (v_ancient_languages < 3)
choice("Give them the two copper pieces", Ch5-Goblins2, v_current_scene = Ch5-Goblins2, v_ch5_goblins = 1) if (v_ancient_languages == 3)
choice("Don't give them anything and send them on their way.", Ch5-Goblins2, v_current_scene = Ch5-Goblins2, v_ch5_goblins = 2) if (v_ancient_languages == 3)


ID: Ch5-Goblins2
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch5_deed,1) if (v_ch5_goblins == 1)
achievement("A good deed",v_ac_ch5_deed)
#if(v_ch5_goblins == 1) {
I dig two copper coins from my pocket and toss them through the air. The goblin leader snatches them against his chest, staring down at the metal as though holding lightning.

"What trick is this?" he demands, eyes wide.

"Two coppers. Your tribute. Return to your clan and stop ambushing travelers."

"A trap! It must be a trap! Do not listen to him!"

"Um... Excuse me... mister goblin?"

A melodic, gentle voice cuts in from behind.

I turn toward the speaker, and the sight of her stops me cold. Long auburn hair cascades over the shoulders of an auburn dress, practical and dust-streaked from the trail. She has a slender waist, soft, delicate features that contrast sharply with Kate's guarded severity, and warm light-brown eyes that carry an immediate, unvarnished kindness. Mid-twenties, holding an herbalist's woven satchel smelling of damp earth and crushed mint. In her open palm, she holds several plump blue berries.

"I overheard you mention trouble telling edible berries from toxic ones," she says, stepping forward without a trace of fear. "I thought I might offer some guidance."

"You... understand our speech too?"

"A little, though not as fluently as the gentleman beside me. Enough to understand why you were desperate. I'm an herbalist by trade, so I know local plants well. I cannot teach you everything in a day, but here is a simple rule: stick to blue berries. They grow across the continent, and even the worst of them cause nothing more than a stomach ache. To find the safe ones, choose the deep, dark blue. Only the pale, light blue berries are dangerous."

She rolls a dark blue berry between her fingers, pops it into her mouth, and chews to demonstrate. Then she unties a small cloth pouch of them from her belt and tosses it to the leader.

"Here. You have far more use for these than I do—I've already eaten. And please... do not attack defenseless travelers anymore."

}
#if(v_ch5_goblins == 2) {
"I understand the situation," I say in their tongue. "Now clear out."

"We will trouble you no further, human. We are not reckless enough to challenge armed warriors."

As he turns to retreat into the thicket, a gentle voice calls out from behind.

"Um... Excuse me... mister goblin?"

I turn toward the speaker, and the sight of her stops me cold. Long auburn hair cascades over the shoulders of an auburn dress, practical and dust-streaked from the trail. She has a slender waist, soft, delicate features that contrast sharply with Kate's guarded severity, and warm light-brown eyes that carry an immediate, unvarnished kindness. Mid-twenties, holding an herbalist's woven satchel smelling of damp earth and crushed mint. In her open palm, she holds several plump blue berries.

"I overheard you mention trouble telling edible berries from toxic ones," she says, stepping forward without a trace of fear. "I thought I might offer some guidance."

"You... understand our speech too?"

"A little, though not as fluently as the gentleman beside me. Enough to understand why you were desperate. I'm an herbalist by trade, so I know local plants well. I cannot teach you everything in a day, but here is a simple rule: stick to blue berries. They grow across the continent, and even the worst of them cause nothing more than a stomach ache. To find the safe ones, choose the deep, dark blue. Only the pale, light blue berries are dangerous."

She rolls a dark blue berry between her fingers, pops it into her mouth, and chews to demonstrate. Then she unties a small cloth pouch of them from her belt and tosses it to the leader.

"Here. You have far more use for these than I do—I've already eaten. And please... do not attack defenseless travelers anymore."

}
choice("Continue", Ch5-Goblins3, v_current_scene = Ch5-Goblins3)


ID: Ch5-Goblins3
TEXT:

#if(v_ch5_goblins == 1) {
The goblin leader stands frozen. Trembling, he lifts a dark berry from the pouch and eats it. As the sweetness registers and his throat remains clear, tears spill down his leathery green cheeks. One of his companions throws an arm around his shoulders, both of them weeping openly.

Goblins weeping over fruit and two copper coins. The auburn-haired woman watches them with a warm, radiant smile. There is genuine joy on her face for the very creatures that had her cornered minutes ago.

Pulling himself together, the goblin leader wipes his face and turns to us.

"Humans, we will never forget this mercy. I, Uluk Mihtun, vow to repay this debt to both of you. We will cross paths again. Until that day..."

He presses his left hand flat against his abdomen and his right hand over his heart. The other three immediately mirror the posture, chanting in unison:

"Tunak Madar!"

Tunak Madar. According to the ancient linguistics in my stat booster, it is the highest honorific of gratitude in their language—an oath of lifelong reverence with no direct equivalent in modern tongues.

As the four goblins slip into the shadows of the forest, I turn around. Daren is rubbing his temple, brow knit in total confusion, while Kate stares with her jaw slightly slack.

"All right," Daren says slowly. "Would someone care to explain what just happened?"

Rose lets out a soft, musical laugh.

"Forgive me," she says. "It must have looked bizarre without understanding our words. They were banished from their clan two days ago over an unpaid tribute, resorting to theft so they wouldn't starve. We simply helped them get back on their feet! But where are my manners? My name is Rose. Thank you all from the bottom of my heart—I don't know what I would have done without you!"

}
#if(v_ch5_goblins == 2) {
The goblin leader stands frozen. Trembling, he lifts a dark berry from the pouch and eats it. As the sweetness registers and his throat remains clear, tears spill down his leathery green cheeks. One of his companions throws an arm around his shoulders, both of them weeping openly.

Goblins weeping over fruit. The auburn-haired woman watches them with a warm, radiant smile. There is genuine joy on her face for the very creatures that had her cornered minutes ago.

Pulling himself together, the goblin leader drops to both knees and drives his crude dagger into the loam. The other three drop beside him in identical submission.

"We are ashamed," he rasps. "You grant us mercy we did not earn. We have nothing to offer but our pleas for forgiveness."

Rose's cheeks flush with gentle embarrassment.

"Please," she says, stepping forward to gesture them up. "There is no need for apologies. You did what you had to in order to survive."

"We are unworthy of such grace. I, Uluk Mihtun, vow to repay this debt. We will cross paths again. Until that day..."

He presses his left hand flat against his abdomen and his right hand over his heart. The other three mirror the posture, chanting in unison:

"Tunak Madar!"

Tunak Madar. According to the ancient linguistics in my stat booster, it is the highest honorific of gratitude in their language—an oath of lifelong reverence with no direct equivalent in modern tongues.

As the four goblins slip into the shadows of the forest, I turn around. Daren is rubbing his temple, brow knit in total confusion, while Kate stares with her jaw slightly slack.

"All right," Daren says slowly. "Would someone care to explain what just happened?"

Rose lets out a soft, musical laugh.

"Forgive me," she says. "It must have looked bizarre without understanding our words. They were banished from their clan two days ago over an unpaid tribute, resorting to theft so they wouldn't starve. I simply offered what little aid I could! But where are my manners? My name is Rose. Thank you all from the bottom of my heart—I don't know what I would have done without you!"

}
choice(""All in the day's work!"", Ch5-Work, v_current_scene = Ch5-Work)
choice(""You were lucky that we managed to make it in time."", Ch5-Lucky, v_current_scene = Ch5-Lucky)
choice(""No problem. We love helping people. But we also accept donations!"", Ch5-Donations, v_current_scene = Ch5-Donations)


ID: Ch5-Work
TEXT:

"All in the day's work!" I tell her with an easy shrug.

"Oh?" Rose's eyebrows lift, a playful spark warming her light-brown eyes. "I take it you joined this tournament purely out of charitable goodwill?"

"Well... I, uh..."

"Pay Barry no mind," Daren chuckles, stepping forward. "He loves puffing his chest around pretty women. As you've probably gathered, we're contestants in the tournament. We heard your shout from our camp and came running. Anyone with a conscience would have done the same."

"You are far too modest, mister swordsman. Many would have hesitated over the non-interference decree, assuming they cared to intervene at all. I would offer a reward for your trouble, but I carry no coin out here—bandits are too common along the borders."

"If I may ask," Kate cuts in, stepping up beside me, "what were you doing out here in the wilderness alone?"

"Well, miss, err..."

"Kate," Kate says with a short gesture toward her own chest, then indicates me and Daren in turn, supplying our names without lingering on formalities.

Rose offers a warm nod.

"Miss Kate, I was searching for rare medicinal flora native to this ridge. An outbreak of oildrip has struck my hometown, and as an herbalist, I know which roots and leaves produce the remedy."

"What is oildrip?" Kate asks.

"Ah, I forgot the ailment doesn't exist across the ocean. The physicians remain uncertain of the root cause, but patients develop a burning fever, violent coughing fits, and sweat as thick and dark as lamp oil. Some possess natural resistance, but those without it are quarantined in isolated wards and left to waste away. The only known cure requires extracts from plants that grow exclusively along these hills."

"Why come yourself?" Kate presses, eyes narrowing slightly. "Are your own kin infected? Why not hire guards to escort you?"

choice("Continue", Ch5-Creator, v_current_scene = Ch5-Creator)


ID: Ch5-Lucky
TEXT:

"You were lucky that we managed to make it in time," I say, crossing my arms. "What were you thinking, wandering into monster territory by yourself?"

"Barry, easy," Daren frowns. "No need to scold her."

"No, he speaks the truth," Rose says softly, looking down at her dust-coated hands. "It was reckless. But time was slipping away, and no one else was willing to make the trek. I couldn't sit by while people suffered."

"If I may ask," Kate cuts in, stepping up beside me, "what were you doing out here in the wilderness alone?"

"Well, miss, err..."

"Kate," Kate says with a short gesture toward her own chest, then indicates me and Daren in turn, supplying our names without lingering on formalities.

Rose offers a warm nod.

"Miss Kate, I was searching for rare medicinal flora native to this ridge. An outbreak of oildrip has struck my hometown, and as an herbalist, I know which roots and leaves produce the remedy."

"What is oildrip?" Kate asks.

"Ah, I forgot the ailment doesn't exist across the ocean. The physicians remain uncertain of the root cause, but patients develop a burning fever, violent coughing fits, and sweat as thick and dark as lamp oil. Some possess natural resistance, but those without it are quarantined in isolated wards and left to waste away. The only known cure requires extracts from plants that grow exclusively along these hills."

"Why come yourself?" Kate presses, eyes narrowing slightly. "Are your own kin infected? Why not hire guards to escort you?"

choice("Continue", Ch5-Creator, v_current_scene = Ch5-Creator)


ID: Ch5-Donations
TEXT:

"No problem. We love helping people. But we also accept donations!"

"Barry, for the love of the gods!" Daren groans, clapping a gauntlet over his face.

Rose lets out a rich, genuine laugh that ripples through the clearing.

"It's quite all right—I take no offense at honest commerce. I would gladly offer a bounty for the rescue, but I left my purse at home. Carrying gold on these trails is an invitation to every cutthroat on the continent."

"If I may ask," Kate cuts in, stepping up beside me, "what were you doing out here in the wilderness alone?"

"Well, miss, err..."

"Kate," Kate says with a short gesture toward her own chest, then indicates me and Daren in turn, supplying our names without lingering on formalities.

Rose offers a warm nod.

"Miss Kate, I was searching for rare medicinal flora native to this ridge. An outbreak of oildrip has struck my hometown, and as an herbalist, I know which roots and leaves produce the remedy."

"What is oildrip?" Kate asks.

"Ah, I forgot the ailment doesn't exist across the ocean. The physicians remain uncertain of the root cause, but patients develop a burning fever, violent coughing fits, and sweat as thick and dark as lamp oil. Some possess natural resistance, but those without it are quarantined in isolated wards and left to waste away. The only known cure requires extracts from plants that grow exclusively along these hills."

"Why come yourself?" Kate presses, eyes narrowing slightly. "Are your own kin infected? Why not hire guards to escort you?"

choice("Continue", Ch5-Creator, v_current_scene = Ch5-Creator)


ID: Ch5-Creator
TEXT:

"My siblings have not been infected—not yet." She steadies herself, one hand pressing flat against the flap of her satchel. "But my father is bedridden. A wasting illness, years now—he cannot leave the house. His sickroom shares a wall with the quarantine ward. I have watched oildrip overwhelm three clinics this season alone. Containment always fails once the cots are full, and when it breaks, my father will not survive the night. He has no natural resistance left."

She draws a breath, straightening.

"Beyond that, the infected are laborers and paupers—people who cannot afford physician fees, let alone hire escorts to forage rare roots for them. Nor do I possess the funds to hire mercenaries myself. I am not a physician, but I volunteer in the infirmaries whenever I can. Since our high priest passed, the casualty numbers climb with every sunrise. He was the last person in our town who possessed the art of conjuring food from thin air through magic. Despite years of effort, none of his acolytes could master the technique. Without him, famine spreads. People eat poisonous berries from desperation, others starve, and some have resorted to... to..."

Her voice catches, sorrow shadowing her eyes.

"...hunting wild beasts," she finishes in a hushed whisper.

"Wait," Daren blinks, taken aback. "Is it truly forbidden to hunt game on this continent?"

"It... was. Centuries ago, every major city in Varathia maintained at least a dozen mages capable of conjuring boundless nourishment from the magical plane. The Creator personally taught that art to the first generation, and they passed it down to their students. But the talent required for food conjuration is exceptionally rare. Ever since Varathia's founding six hundred years ago, our civilization relied on the Creator to identify gifted disciples and train them. But the Creator vanished a century ago, and with each passing generation, the masters of the craft die out."

"Pardon the ignorance," Daren says, "but who exactly is the Creator?"

"Forgive me. I forget how little history crosses the sea. The Creator founded our entire civilization. Everything we built, we owe to his design. Six centuries ago, he and the golden fox—the great guardian spirit of all animals—forged a pact to banish war and starvation forever. They united human and animal realms under a shared peace, teaching magic that conjured food, clean water, heat, and shelter from thin air. Labor and conflict vanished. From all the chronicles tell, it was paradise on earth."

choice(""Why did the Creator disappear?"", Ch5-Disappear, v_current_scene = Ch5-Disappear)
choice(""How did the Creator live so long?"", Ch5-Long, v_current_scene = Ch5-Long)


ID: Ch5-Disappear
TEXT:

"Why did the Creator disappear?"

"No one knows for certain," Rose says, adjusting the strap of her herbal satchel. "Legends abound, though proof is scarce. Some claim madness took him in his final decades and he walked into the wastes without a word. Others say he grew disgusted with mortal failings and abandoned us. A few insist he simply grew bored and departed across the ocean to build another kingdom. Most believe he died of old age, and the royal houses concealed his death to forestall panic. As long as common folk cling to the hope that their founder might return, public order holds."

She catches herself and offers a self-deprecating smile.

"Listen to me, burdening you with local folklore after you saved my life. I only wish I had something of substance to offer in return."

Daren grins broadly.

"You'll have plenty of time to brainstorm on the road—we're escorting you home."

"What?!" Kate snaps, rounding on him.

choice(""That sounds like a great idea."", Ch5-Great, v_current_scene = Ch5-Great)
choice(""Don't you think you should consult us before making such a decision?"", Ch5-Consult, v_current_scene = Ch5-Consult)


ID: Ch5-Long
TEXT:

"How did the Creator live so long? From what you're saying, he reigned for centuries."

"He did," Rose nods. "Which is why skepticism has festered in recent decades. A century has passed since he was last seen, and even in his prime, he rarely appeared before common citizens—mostly dealing with royal councils. Dissenters now claim the Creator was merely a fable concocted by the monarchs to hoard the secret of food conjuration. In several southern provinces, factions have taken up arms against their crowns over the shortage, pushing whole regions toward civil war."

She catches herself and offers a self-deprecating smile.

"Listen to me, burdening you with local folklore after you saved my life. I only wish I had something of substance to offer in return."

Daren grins broadly.

"You'll have plenty of time to brainstorm on the road—we're escorting you home."

"What?!" Kate snaps, rounding on him.

choice(""That sounds like a great idea."", Ch5-Great, v_current_scene = Ch5-Great)
choice(""Don't you think you should consult us before making such a decision?"", Ch5-Consult, v_current_scene = Ch5-Consult)


ID: Ch5-Great
TEXT:

"That sounds like a great idea."

Kate glares at me, frustration flaring in her dark eyes. "You too?"

"We have nothing to do until the organizers announce the tournament objectives," I point out. "And a fortified town is far safer than sleeping in a cave where contestants ambush us every few hours. Besides, you don't have an active lead on your friend's location yet, do you?"

"I... no, but—"

"Then it's settled!" Daren beams.

"Nothing is settled!" Kate shoots back. "I never agreed to escort duty."

"Please, don't quarrel on my account," Rose steps between them, palms raised gently. "Daren, I appreciate your generosity, but I won't cause strife among companions. I know these trails well. I map my foraging routes strictly around known beast nests and bandit crossings. Those goblins were an isolated pack of exiles far from their clan grounds. I can reach Thilias safely on my own."

"Quiet," Kate whispers, her hand dropping to her side. "Do you hear that?"

The forest falls still. Through the pines drifts a jaunty, careless whistling—an unfamiliar tune, echoing closer with every step.

The whistling cuts out as three figures step into the clearing. Mages, judging by the enchantments shimmering along their armaments. Cleaner and better equipped than Cutthroat Dave's crew, but carrying the same predatory arrogance.

"Clive, look at that," one calls out, pointing a gloved hand toward our group.

"By the gods," another breathes, stumbling back a step. "A banshee."

The third man shrieks, losing his footing and landing flat in the dirt.

"Monster! Cut it down before it shrieks!" terror cracks Clive's voice.

Kate's head drops. Her shoulders tense, but the fire in her eyes isn't anger—it's raw, exhausted misery.

Clive draws his short sword and charges straight at Kate, screaming a battle cry to drown out his panic. Daren reaches for his blade, but before he can step across, a flash of auburn dress cuts across the grass.

Rose places herself directly in Clive's path, arms spread wide.

choice("Continue", Ch5-Monster, v_current_scene = Ch5-Monster)


ID: Ch5-Consult
TEXT:

"Don't you think you should consult us before making such a decision?"

"Come now, Barry!" Daren gestures toward the trees. "A town offers warm beds and sturdy walls while we wait for the tournament directives. Isn't that better than camping in the dirt?"

"Reaching a city on our own is fine. Escorting a local across the province is another matter entirely. Rescuing her from monsters is defensible; playing personal bodyguards treads uncomfortably close to foreign interference."

"We'll simply register her as our local guide. Relax, it's completely above board."

"I agree with Barry," Kate says firmly. "We shouldn't waste daylight escorting civilians. The judges could announce the first round at any moment."

"Please, don't quarrel on my account," Rose steps between them, palms raised gently. "Daren, I appreciate your generosity, but I won't cause strife among companions. I know these trails well. I map my foraging routes strictly around known beast nests and bandit crossings. Those goblins were an isolated pack of exiles far from their clan grounds. I can reach Thilias safely on my own."

"Quiet," Kate whispers, her hand dropping to her side. "Do you hear that?"

The forest falls still. Through the pines drifts a jaunty, careless whistling—an unfamiliar tune, echoing closer with every step.

The whistling cuts out as three figures step into the clearing. Mages, judging by the enchantments shimmering along their armaments. Cleaner and better equipped than Cutthroat Dave's crew, but carrying the same predatory arrogance.

"Clive, look at that," one calls out, pointing a gloved hand toward our group.

"By the gods," another breathes, stumbling back a step. "A banshee."

The third man shrieks, losing his footing and landing flat in the dirt.

"Monster! Cut it down before it shrieks!" terror cracks Clive's voice.

Kate's head drops. Her shoulders tense, but the fire in her eyes isn't anger—it's raw, exhausted misery.

Clive draws his short sword and charges straight at Kate, screaming a battle cry to drown out his panic. Daren reaches for his blade, but before he can step across, a flash of auburn dress cuts across the grass.

Rose places herself directly in Clive's path, arms spread wide.

choice("Continue", Ch5-Monster, v_current_scene = Ch5-Monster)


ID: Ch5-Monster
TEXT:

"Halt!" Rose commands, her voice ringing clear and authoritative across the grass.

Clive skids to a stop, blade trembling inches from her chest.

"Out of the way, girl," he snarls. "Step aside or I'll run you through alongside that abomination!"

"You will not harm her. I owe her my life. And you cannot touch me—I am a citizen of Varathia under full protection of the tournament decrees!"

"You think rules hold me back?" Clive sneers, lips curling back from yellowed teeth. "I've killed men before. Here's a trade secret: without witnesses, there's no proof. You don't actually believe that nonsense about an automatic spell whisking me away before steel bites, do you? Any mage with half a brain knows it's an empty bluff."

He isn't entirely wrong. From an enchantment standpoint, a ward that preempts physical violence before an intent manifests would require continuous, continent-wide mind-reading.

"Clive," his companion calls from the back, voice wavering. "Leave it. We shouldn't butcher locals on day two."

"I'm not butchering her," Clive grins viciously. "I'm just putting an insolent little girl in her place!"

"No!" Daren roars, lunging forward with his shield raised.

At the same instant, Kate thrusts her palm toward Clive. Frost crackles through the air as a jagged cone of ice condenses between her fingers.

Clive slashes his dagger straight at Rose's throat.

Rose shuts her eyes tight, bracing for the impact.

The steel stops three inches from her skin, frozen in midair. Clive's entire frame seizes, vibrating violently as a blinding pillar of white radiance erupts around him.

"Ah, shi—"

The light implodes, and Clive vanishes from the clearing—cast off the continent and stripped of his tournament standing.

"What did you do to Clive, you filthy—"

A crack like a whip snaps across the clearing. Kate's ice spike punches clean through the second mage's right shoulder, pinning muscle and bone under frosted blood.

The man howls, dropping to one knee as crimson stains his leather tunic.

"Under ordinary circumstances, I'd end you where you stand," Kate's voice is dead, flat ice. "Consider this your only warning. Take your wounded comrade and vanish before I change my mind."

The wounded mage clutches his shattered shoulder, grinding his teeth in agony.

"Forget the banshee!" he barks to his trembling ally. "Run!"

They scramble through the brush, their panicked crashing fading into the woods.

Once the forest quiets, Kate turns to Rose, lowering her hand.

"I take back my earlier words. It would be an honor to escort you to your hometown. What you did was remarkably brave."

choice(""Really? Because to me it just seemed reckless and stupid."", Ch5-Reckless, v_current_scene = Ch5-Reckless)
choice(""Since when do you appreciate bravery and heroism?"", Ch5-Heroism, v_current_scene = Ch5-Heroism)


ID: Ch5-Reckless
TEXT:

"Really? Because to me it just seemed reckless and stupid."

A sharp smack lands on the back of my skull.

"What was that for?" I rub the sore spot, glaring at Kate.

"Do you lack every shred of tact?"

"I have plenty. At least I don't assault teammates unprovoked."

"Keep talking and I'll give you another knot to balance it out."

A soft, melodic giggle breaks between us.

Rose stands with one hand over her mouth, her light-brown eyes dancing with amusement.

"Forgive me," she says, lowering her hand. "You remind me so much of my younger brother and sister when they bicker over supper. You two must be very close."

A flush rises along Kate's cheekbones, dusting her fair skin with pink. It's the first time I've seen her blush since we met yesterday. It softens her sharp angles considerably.

"I can certainly see it," Daren grins, clapping a heavy gauntlet onto my shoulder. "Barry's been utterly captivated by Kate since the moment he laid eyes on her."

"You were what?!" Kate rounds on me, ice in her voice and heat in her cheeks.

"Hold that thought for camp tonight!" Daren laughs, waving his hands to cut off the explosion. "With Rose in our company, we need to restructure our travel formation. We cannot afford to let monsters or rogue mages breach our perimeter on the road to Thilias."

We spend the remainder of the morning drilling tactical formations, adjusting for Rose's position at our center before packing our gear.

choice("Next chapter", Ch6-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch6-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: Ch5-Heroism
TEXT:

"Since when do you appreciate bravery and heroism?"

A solid punch catches me squarely in the right shoulder. The impact carries enough force to guarantee a purple bruise by nightfall.

"Damn it, Kate. Ever heard of restraint?"

"Shut up! You earned that. I was trying to offer a genuine compliment and you ruined the moment."

"Politeness doesn't suit you anyway. Punching people, on the other hand..."

A soft, melodic giggle breaks between us.

Rose stands with one hand over her mouth, her light-brown eyes dancing with amusement.

"Forgive me," she says, lowering her hand. "You remind me so much of my younger brother and sister when they bicker over supper. You two must be very close."

A flush rises along Kate's cheekbones, dusting her fair skin with pink. It's the first time I've seen her blush since we met yesterday. It softens her sharp angles considerably.

"I can certainly see it," Daren grins, clapping a heavy gauntlet onto my shoulder. "Barry's been utterly captivated by Kate since the moment he laid eyes on her."

"You were what?!" Kate rounds on me, ice in her voice and heat in her cheeks.

"Hold that thought for camp tonight!" Daren laughs, waving his hands to cut off the explosion. "With Rose in our company, we need to restructure our travel formation. We cannot afford to let monsters or rogue mages breach our perimeter on the road to Thilias."

We spend the remainder of the morning drilling tactical formations, adjusting for Rose's position at our center before packing our gear.

